          "type": "output_text",
          "annotations": [],
          "logprobs": [],
          "text": "{\"target_translation\":\"I love you. (Here, Cordelia addresses her father formally: “I love your majesty.”)\",\"dimensions\":{\"T\":{\"score\":4,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.98,\"reason\":\"Cordelia directly avows a loving state toward Lear. She presents her love as genuine but limited to the measure appropriate to their father-daughter relationship.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"target\",\"quotation\":\"I love you\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"I love your majesty\\nAccording to my bond; no more nor less.\"}]},\"P\":{\"score\":2,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.88,\"reason\":\"The expression invokes an existing filial bond and its obligations rather than creating a new promise. Cordelia identifies herself as loving Lear according to the duty she owes him as his daughter; the surrounding explanation specifies obedience, love, and honour as duties she returns to him.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"According to my bond; no more nor less.\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"Return those duties back as are right fit,\\nObey you, love you, and most honour you.\"}]},\"E\":{\"score\":0,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.94,\"reason\":\"The love expression is a deliberate, measured answer to Lear’s demand, not a reflex-like affective discharge. Cordelia explicitly regulates what she says and characterizes it in terms of proportion and duty.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"target\",\"quotation\":\"I love you\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"I love your majesty\\nAccording to my bond; no more nor less.\"}]},\"O\":{\"score\":1,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.76,\"reason\":\"Beyond avowal and invocation of filial obligation, the expression functions weakly as a boundary-setting refusal to participate in Lear’s demand for unlimited declarations. Cordelia answers while rejecting the premise that a daughter should profess all-encompassing love for her father.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"no more nor less.\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"Why have my sisters husbands if they say\\nThey love you all?\"}]}},\"original_speech_situation\":\"Lear publicly asks his daughters to say which loves him most, linking their answers to the division of his kingdom. After initially answering “Nothing,” Cordelia states that she loves him according to her filial bond, neither more nor less. Lear treats this measured answer as inadequate and warns that it may damage her fortunes.\",\"affection_evidence\":\"present\",\"undertaking_and_uptake\":\"Cordelia does not make a new promise. She invokes her existing filial obligations to Lear—described as obedience, love, and honour—and limits them to what is fitting. Lear does not accept this formulation as a satisfactory response to his love test and urges her to revise it.\",\"recognised_work\":true,\"recognition_note\":\"The supplied text is recognisable as William Shakespeare’s King Lear. The classification relies only on the supplied passages.\",\"limitations\":\"The target modernizes and shortens Cordelia’s actual wording, omitting the crucial qualification “According to my bond; no more nor less.” The supplied later passages strongly support actual affection through her concern, care, and grief, but sincerity is analytically separate from the avowing force classified under T.\"}"
        }
      ],
